add({id:"da-adjust", dom:"Data", vol:"15.2", name:"Split and dividend adjustment", t:[60,75,95], gen(R,tier){
  const P=rf(R,20,400,2), split=pick(R,[2,3,4,5,7,10]), div=rf(R,0.2,4,2);
  const adj=(P-div)/split;
  return {q:`A stock closes at <b>$${f2(P)}</b>. Overnight it goes ex-dividend <b>$${f2(div)}</b> and then splits <b>${split}-for-1</b>.<br>
What is the previous close, restated on today's adjusted basis? (to 4 dp)`,
    a:adj, tol:0.0004, rel:true, unit:"$",
    hint:"Subtract the dividend, then divide by the split factor.",
    sol:`Ex-dividend: ${f2(P)} − ${f2(div)} = ${f2(P-div)}.<br>
Post-split: ${f2(P-div)} ÷ ${split} = <b>$${f2(adj,4)}</b>.<br>
Get the order or the operation wrong and you have manufactured a ${f2((P/split-adj)/adj*100,2)}% return that never happened. Do it once in a long history and a backtest will find it, size into it, and report a wonderful Sharpe. Checking the twenty largest absolute returns in any new dataset against a news source is twenty minutes that saves months.`};
}});

add({id:"da-survivor", dom:"Data", vol:"0.8", name:"Size of the survivorship bias", t:[60,75,95], gen(R,tier){
  const n=pick(R,[100,500,1000,3000]), fail=pick(R,[2,4,6,8]), yrs=pick(R,[5,10,20]);
  const surv=Math.pow(1-fail/100,yrs);
  const missing=(1-surv)*100;
  return {q:`A universe of <b>${n}</b> names loses <b>${fail}%</b> of its members each year to delisting or failure.<br>
After <b>${yrs}</b> years, what percentage of the original universe is missing from a list of today's survivors? (to 2 dp, %)`,
    a:missing, tol:0.05, unit:"%",
    sol:`Survival = (1 − ${fail/100})^${yrs} = ${f2(surv,4)}.<br>
Missing = <b>${f2(missing)}%</b> — that is <b>${f2(n*(1-surv),0)}</b> names your backtest never sees.<br>
And they are not a random sample: they are the ones that fell furthest. A momentum strategy backtested on survivors never buys the falling knife that went to zero, and a value strategy never buys the cheap company that was cheap for a reason. The bias always flatters, which is why it survives review.`};
}});

add({id:"da-pit", dom:"Data", vol:"15.1", name:"Restatement lag and point-in-time", t:[55,70,90], gen(R,tier){
  const qEnd=pick(R,["31 March","30 June","30 September","31 December"]);
  const lag=pick(R,[30,45,60,75,90]);
  const rev=pick(R,[180,270,365,540]);
  return {q:`A company's quarter ends <b>${qEnd}</b>. It files <b>${lag}</b> days later and restates the figure <b>${rev}</b> days after the quarter end.<br>
How many days after the quarter end may a point-in-time backtest first use the <em>original</em> figure?`,
    a:lag, tol:0.001, unit:"days", int:true,
    sol:`<b>${lag} days</b> — the filing date, not the quarter end, and not the restatement.<br>
A vendor database that stores only the current value will hand you the restated figure and date it to ${qEnd}. That gives your backtest ${lag} days of foresight on the original and ${rev} days on the revision.<br>
The test for point-in-time data is mechanical: on that date, at that hour, could I have known this exact number? Most vendor data fails it silently.`};
}});

