add({id:"sp-portopt", dom:"Portfolio", vol:"11.3", name:"The optimiser call", t:[105,130,155], gen(R){
  const n=pick(R,[120,250,400]);
  return {
    stem:`Mean-variance optimisation across <b>${n}</b> assets using two years of daily data.`,
    mono:true,
    lines:[
      {t:"cov = returns.cov() * 252", bad:1,
       why:`A sample covariance for ${n} assets from about 500 observations. With assets comparable to observations the matrix is near-singular and its inverse is dominated by noise.`},
      {t:"mu = returns.mean() * 252", bad:1,
       why:"Historical mean returns as expected returns. Mean estimates are so noisy that mean-variance portfolios built on them reliably underperform equal weighting; this is the single most documented failure in portfolio construction."},
      {t:"inv = np.linalg.inv(cov)"},
      {t:"w = inv @ mu"},
      {t:"w = w / w.sum()"},
      {t:"# no constraints", bad:1,
       why:"Unconstrained weights on a noisy inverse covariance produce enormous offsetting long-short positions. Position limits are not a view — they are insurance against estimation error."},
      {t:"allocate(w)"}],
    close:`Every flaw here is a way of trusting an estimate more than the data supports. The fixes are well established and unglamorous: shrink or denoise the covariance, replace the mean estimates with something structural or drop them entirely (minimum variance needs no means), and constrain the weights. A constrained minimum-variance portfolio on a shrunk matrix beats this out of sample almost every time.`};
}});

add({id:"sp-monte", dom:"Probability", vol:"23.4", name:"A Monte Carlo of the equity curve", t:[110,135,160], gen(R){
  return {
    stem:`Simulating possible futures for a strategy to estimate drawdown risk.`,
    mono:true,
    lines:[
      {t:"mu, sd = returns.mean(), returns.std()"},
      {t:"paths = []"},
      {t:"for _ in range(10_000):"},
      {t:"    r = np.random.normal(mu, sd, 252)", bad:1,
       why:"Normal draws from the sample moments. Financial returns have fat tails and volatility clustering; a normal simulation understates the drawdowns you are trying to estimate."},
      {t:"    paths.append(np.cumprod(1 + r))"},
      {t:"dd = [max_drawdown(p) for p in paths]"},
      {t:"print('95th percentile drawdown', np.percentile(dd, 95))"},
      {t:"# mu treated as known", bad:1,
       why:"The mean is estimated with enormous error and is fed in as if certain. Sampling mu from its own posterior widens the outcome distribution far more than any tail adjustment."},
      {t:"# independent draws each period", bad:1,
       why:"Independent returns cannot produce volatility clustering, so no simulated path has a bad month followed by a worse one — which is what real drawdowns are made of."}],
    close:`The simulation says the worst plausible drawdown is smaller than it is, in three separate ways, all pointing the same direction. The cheapest fix is not a fancier model — it is a <b>block bootstrap of your own returns</b>, which preserves fat tails and clustering for free, plus resampling mu from its confidence interval to show how much of the uncertainty is simply not knowing your own edge.`};
}});

