add({id:"mc-tickval", dom:"Mechanics", vol:"02.1", name:"Tick value and position P&L", t:[40,50,65], gen(R,tier){
  const mult=pick(R,[5,10,20,50,100,1000]), tick=pick(R,[0.01,0.25,0.5,1]);
  const n=ri(R,1,40), ticks=ri(R,-60,60);
  const pnl=n*ticks*tick*mult;
  return {q:`A futures contract has a multiplier of <b>${mult}</b> and a minimum tick of <b>${tick}</b>. You hold <b>${n}</b> contracts and the price moves <b>${ticks}</b> ticks — positive in your favour, negative against you.<br>What is the P&L?`,
    a:pnl, tol:0.005, rel:true, unit:"$",
    sol:`Tick value = ${tick} × ${mult} = $${f2(tick*mult,2)} per contract.<br>
P&L = ${n} × ${ticks} × $${f2(tick*mult,2)} = <b>${money2(pnl)}</b>.<br>
The tick value is the number to know before trading any contract — it converts a price move into money, and it is the reason a "small" move in one contract is a large one in another.`};
}});

add({id:"mc-margin2", dom:"Mechanics", vol:"19A.4", name:"How far to a margin call", t:[45,55,70], gen(R,tier){
  const A=ri(R,10,120,5)*1000, lev=pick(R,[1.5,2,3,4,6]), mm=pick(R,[25,30,35,40]);
  const drop=(1/lev-mm/100)/(1-mm/100);
  return {q:`Account equity <b>${money(A)}</b>, gross exposure <b>${lev}×</b> equity, maintenance margin <b>${mm}%</b>.<br>What percentage fall in the positions triggers a margin call? (to 1 dp, %)`,
    a:drop*100, tol:0.15, rel:true, unit:"%",
    hint:"Equity falls faster than the position: solve for when equity/value hits the maintenance requirement.",
    sol:`Position value = ${money(A*lev)}, borrowed = ${money(A*(lev-1))}.<br>
A fall of x leaves equity ${money(A)} − ${money(A*lev)}·x against a value of ${money(A*lev)}(1−x); setting that ratio to ${mm}% gives x = <b>${pc(drop*100,1)}</b>.<br>
At ${lev}× a <b>${pc(drop*100,1)}</b> move brings the call. Note how quickly this shrinks: at ${lev*2}× it would be about ${pc(((1/(lev*2)-mm/100)/(1-mm/100))*100,1)}.`};
}});

/* ================= PROBABILITY ================= */

add({id:"pb-bayes2", dom:"Probability", vol:"23.2", name:"Updating on a run of wins", t:[55,65,80], gen(R,tier){
  const prior=pick(R,[0.1,0.2,0.3,0.5]);
  const n=ri(R,4,10), pSkill=pick(R,[0.6,0.65,0.7]);
  const lr=Math.pow(pSkill/0.5,n);
  const post=prior*lr/(prior*lr+(1-prior));
  return {q:`Before seeing any results you thought there was a <b>${pc(prior*100,0)}</b> chance a trader has skill. A skilled trader wins <b>${pc(pSkill*100,0)}</b> of trades; an unskilled one wins 50%.<br>They win <b>${n}</b> trades in a row. What is the probability they are skilled? (to 1 dp, %)`,
    a:post*100, tol:1.0, unit:"%",
    hint:"Likelihood ratio = (p_skill/0.5)^n, then apply Bayes.",
    sol:`LR = (${f2(pSkill,2)}/0.5)^${n} = ${f2(lr,2)}.<br>
Posterior = ${f2(prior,2)}×${f2(lr,2)} / (${f2(prior,2)}×${f2(lr,2)} + ${f2(1-prior,2)}) = <b>${pc(post*100,1)}</b>.<br>
${post<0.6?`Still under 60% after ${n} straight wins — which is the point. Short runs move a prior far less than intuition insists, because an unskilled trader produces a ${n}-win streak ${pc(Math.pow(0.5,n)*100,2)} of the time and there are many unskilled traders.`:`A substantial update, though note it depended heavily on the prior of ${pc(prior*100,0)}.`}`};
}});

add({id:"pb-ruin", dom:"Probability", vol:"23.6", name:"Risk of ruin", t:[55,65,80], gen(R,tier){
  const p=rf(R,0.5,0.62,2), units=ri(R,5,40);
  const q=1-p;
  const ruin=p<=0.5?1:Math.pow(q/p,units);
  return {q:`You make even-money bets with a <b>${pc(p*100,0)}</b> win probability, risking one unit each time, starting with <b>${units}</b> units.<br>What is the probability of eventually losing everything? (to 4 dp)`,
    a:ruin, tol:0.02, rel:true, unit:"",
    hint:"Risk of ruin = (q/p)^units for even-money bets with p > 0.5.",
    sol:`(${f2(q,2)}/${f2(p,2)})^${units} = <b>${f2(ruin,4)}</b>.<br>
${ruin<0.01?"Effectively safe — with a real edge and enough units, ruin becomes negligible very quickly.":ruin<0.2?"A real risk, and it falls exponentially in the number of units: doubling the bankroll to "+units*2+" units would take it to "+f2(Math.pow(q/p,units*2),4)+".":"A serious probability of ruin. The edge is not the problem; the number of units is."}<br>
The exponent is the whole lesson. Writing p = ½ + ε, (q/p)<sup>N</sup> ≈ e<sup>−4Nε</sup>: ruin falls exponentially in the <b>product</b> of edge and bankroll units, so doubling your units and doubling your edge buy almost exactly the same protection. The asymmetry is not in the arithmetic but in what you control — bet size is a decision you make today, and edge is not.`};
}});

