add({id:"tn-slices", dom:"Execution", vol:"10.5", name:"How many child orders", t:[155,185,215], gen(R){
  return mk(R, R=>{
  const fee=rf(R,0.05,0.6,2);             /* fixed cost per child order, bp of that slice */
  const detect=rf(R,0.4,1.6,2);           /* detectability penalty coefficient */
  const impactTot=rf(R,8,40,1);           /* total impact if done in one go, bp */
  const cost=n=>{
    const impact=impactTot/Math.sqrt(n);           /* splitting reduces per-slice impact */
    const fees=fee*n;                               /* each child costs something */
    const detected=detect*Math.log(1+n)*1.6;        /* more slices, more pattern to detect */
    return impact+fees+detected;
  };
  const o={
    stem:`You must work an order whose impact would be about <b>${f2(impactTot,0)} bp</b> if sent in one piece. Splitting it reduces impact roughly as <b>1/√n</b>. Each child order carries about <b>${f2(fee,2)} bp</b> of fixed cost, and a longer sequence of children is easier for others to detect.`,
    goal:`Choose the <b>number of child orders</b> that minimises total cost. Splitting helps, and it does not help without limit.`,
    hint:"Impact falls as 1/√n. Fixed costs rise linearly with n, and detectability rises roughly with log n.",
    controls:[{n:"n", label:"Child orders", lo:1, hi:200, step:1, init:10, unit:"", dec:0,
      note:"How many pieces the parent is split into."}],
    readout(v){
      return [
        {l:"impact", v:f2(impactTot/Math.sqrt(v.n),1)+" bp"},
        {l:"fixed costs", v:f2(fee*v.n,1)+" bp", tone:fee*v.n>10?"warn":""},
        {l:"detectability", v:f2(detect*Math.log(1+v.n)*1.6,1)+" bp"},
        {l:"total", v:f2(cost(v.n),1)+" bp", tone:cost(v.n)<cost(1)*0.6?"pos":""}];
    },
    chart(v){
      const vals=[]; for(let n=1;n<=200;n++) vals.push(cost(n));
      return {vals, caption:`Total cost against the number of child orders. Your setting: <b>${v.n}</b>.`};
    },
    score(v){ return -cost(v.n); },
    pass:0.92,
    sol:`Impact falls with √n while fixed costs rise linearly, so there is a point past which further splitting costs more than it saves.`,
    close:`The shape is worth internalising because the first few splits do almost all the work: going from one order to four cuts impact in half, while going from a hundred to four hundred cuts it by another half at four times the fixed cost.<br>
The detectability term is the one execution algorithms actually fight over. A long sequence of children is a pattern, and a pattern that can be inferred can be traded ahead of — which is why real algorithms randomise both size and interval rather than simply splitting evenly.`};
  return o; });
}});

add({id:"tn-inventory", dom:"Markets", vol:"02.9", name:"Skew your quotes for inventory", t:[155,185,215], gen(R){
  return mk(R, R=>{
  const inv=ri(R,20,90)*(R()<0.5?1:-1);   /* current inventory, lots */
  const sig=rf(R,0.8,2.4,1);              /* daily vol % */
  const flow=ri(R,20,80);                 /* trades an hour at zero skew */
  const edge=rf(R,0.4,1.6,1);             /* bp captured per trade */
  const invRisk=Math.abs(inv)*sig/100*100;
  const util=sk=>{
    /* skewing attracts flow that reduces inventory and gives up edge on it */
    const reduce=Math.min(Math.abs(inv), flow*sk*0.09);
    const riskCut=reduce*sig/100*100*1.4;
    const edgeLost=flow*edge*sk*0.055;
    const flowLost=flow*edge*Math.pow(sk,1.7)*0.02;
    return riskCut - edgeLost - flowLost;
  };
  const o={
    stem:`You are ${inv>0?"long":"short"} <b>${Math.abs(inv)}</b> lots and the underlying runs at <b>${f2(sig,1)}%</b> daily volatility, so the inventory alone carries about <b>${f2(invRisk,0)} bp</b> of risk. You see roughly <b>${flow}</b> trades an hour and capture about <b>${f2(edge,1)} bp</b> on each.`,
    goal:`Skew your quotes to attract flow that reduces the inventory. Skewing works and it costs edge on every trade you do.`,
    hint:"A larger skew reduces inventory faster and gives up more edge per trade — and eventually drives flow away entirely.",
    controls:[{n:"sk", label:"Quote skew", lo:0, hi:10, step:0.25, init:1, unit:" ticks", dec:2,
      note:"How far you shade quotes toward the side that flattens you."}],
    readout(v){
      const reduce=Math.min(Math.abs(inv), flow*v.sk*0.09);
      return [
        {l:"inventory reduced", v:f2(reduce,0)+" lots", tone:reduce>Math.abs(inv)*0.5?"pos":""},
        {l:"risk removed", v:f2(reduce*sig/100*100*1.4,0)+" bp", tone:"pos"},
        {l:"edge given up", v:f2(flow*edge*v.sk*0.055+flow*edge*Math.pow(v.sk,1.7)*0.02,0)+" bp", tone:"neg"},
        {l:"net", v:f2(util(v.sk),0)+" bp", tone:util(v.sk)>0?"pos":"neg"}];
    },
    chart(v){
      const vals=[]; for(let x=0;x<=10.001;x+=0.25) vals.push(util(x));
      return {vals, base:0, caption:`Net benefit of skewing. Your setting: <b>${f2(v.sk,2)} ticks</b>.`};
    },
    score(v){ return util(v.sk); },
    pass:0.9,
    sol:`Skewing converts edge into inventory reduction. It is worth doing up to the point where the edge given up exceeds the risk removed.`,
    close:`Inventory management is most of what distinguishes a market maker who survives from one who does not. The quote is not just a price — it is the <b>lever that controls the size of your position</b>, and it is the only lever that does not require crossing the spread.<br>
Two consequences. The optimal skew grows with your inventory and with volatility, so it is a function rather than a setting. And when it stops working — when the flow does not come at any skew — that is information: it means everyone is on the same side, and the correct action is to cross the spread and pay to get flat rather than to skew further.`};
  return o; });
}});

