add({id:"tn-blocklen", dom:"Statistics", vol:"04.6", name:"Choose the bootstrap block length", t:[150,180,210], gen(R){
  return mk(R, R=>{
  const rho=pick(R,[0.05,0.15,0.3,0.5]);
  const n=pick(R,[250,500,1000]);
  const acTime=Math.max(1, -1/Math.log(Math.max(rho,0.01)));
  const util=b=>{
    const depBias=Math.exp(-b/Math.max(acTime,0.6))*1.5;   /* too short: dependence destroyed */
    const varPen=b/n*22;                                    /* too long: too few blocks */
    return -(depBias+varPen);
  };
  const o={
    stem:`You are block-bootstrapping <b>${n}</b> returns with a first-order autocorrelation of <b>${f2(rho,2)}</b>, giving a dependence timescale of roughly <b>${f2(acTime,1)}</b> periods.<br>
Blocks that are too short destroy the dependence you are trying to preserve; blocks that are too long leave too few of them to resample.`,
    goal:`Choose the <b>block length</b> that best preserves the series' dependence without exhausting the sample.`,
    hint:"The block must span the autocorrelation, and there must be enough blocks left to resample from.",
    controls:[{n:"b", label:"Block length", lo:1, hi:Math.max(40, Math.round(n/8)), step:1, init:5, unit:" periods", dec:0,
      note:"Contiguous observations per block."}],
    readout(v){
      return [
        {l:"blocks available", v:f2(n/v.b,0), tone:n/v.b<20?"warn":""},
        {l:"dependence kept", v:pc((1-Math.exp(-v.b/Math.max(acTime,0.6)))*100,0), tone:v.b<acTime?"warn":"pos"},
        {l:"variance penalty", v:f2(v.b/n*22,2), tone:v.b/n*22>1?"neg":""},
        {l:"quality", v:f2(util(v.b),3)}];
    },
    chart(v){
      const vals=[]; for(let x=1;x<=Math.max(40,Math.round(n/8));x++) vals.push(util(x));
      return {vals, caption:`Bootstrap quality against block length. Your setting: <b>${v.b}</b>.`};
    },
    score(v){ return util(v.b); },
    pass:0.88,
    sol:`A common rule of thumb is n^(1/3) = ${f2(Math.pow(n,1/3),1)}, adjusted upward when the autocorrelation is strong — here the dependence timescale is about ${f2(acTime,1)} periods.`,
    close:`The reason this matters at all is that the ordinary i.i.d. bootstrap is <b>wrong on financial data and wrong in the flattering direction</b>: resampling individual observations destroys serial correlation and volatility clustering, so the confidence interval comes out far too narrow.<br>
Block bootstrapping is a two-line change that preserves both, and the block length is the one parameter. Get it roughly right — long enough to span the dependence, short enough to leave twenty or more blocks — and the interval becomes honest. Precision in the choice matters far less than making it at all.`};
  return o; });
}});

add({id:"tn-ensemble", dom:"Machine learning", vol:"06.1", name:"How many models to average", t:[150,180,210], gen(R){
  return mk(R, R=>{
  const rho=pick(R,[0.3,0.5,0.7,0.85]);   /* correlation between model errors */
  const cost=rf(R,0.002,0.02,3);          /* complexity/latency cost per model */
  const err=n=>Math.sqrt((1+(n-1)*rho)/n);
  const util=n=>-(err(n) + cost*n);
  const o={
    stem:`You are averaging predictions from several models whose <em>errors</em> correlate at about <b>${f2(rho,2)}</b>. Each additional model adds complexity, latency and maintenance worth roughly <b>${f2(cost,3)}</b> in the same units as the error.`,
    goal:`Choose how many models to ensemble. Averaging reduces error, and the reduction is bounded by how much the models disagree.`,
    hint:"Ensemble error falls as √((1+(n−1)ρ)/n), which is the same formula as portfolio volatility — and it floors at √ρ.",
    controls:[{n:"n", label:"Models in the ensemble", lo:1, hi:30, step:1, init:1, unit:"", dec:0,
      note:"Averaged with equal weight."}],
    readout(v){
      return [
        {l:"ensemble error", v:f2(err(v.n),3), tone:err(v.n)<0.8?"pos":""},
        {l:"floor at ρ", v:f2(Math.sqrt(rho),3)},
        {l:"complexity cost", v:f2(cost*v.n,3), tone:cost*v.n>0.1?"warn":""},
        {l:"total", v:f2(-util(v.n),3)}];
    },
    chart(v){
      const vals=[]; for(let n=1;n<=30;n++) vals.push(util(n));
      return {vals, caption:`Ensemble quality against model count. Your setting: <b>${v.n}</b>.`};
    },
    score(v){ return util(v.n); },
    pass:0.9,
    sol:`With error correlation ${f2(rho,2)}, ensemble error cannot fall below <b>√ρ = ${f2(Math.sqrt(rho),2)}</b> of a single model's, however many you add.`,
    close:`This is the portfolio diversification formula wearing different clothes, and it says the same thing: <b>the benefit is bounded by the correlation, not by the count</b>. At ρ = ${f2(rho,2)} you capture most of the available reduction within about ${Math.max(2,Math.round(1/rho))} models and the rest is maintenance.<br>
Which reframes what ensembling is for. Adding a fifteenth variant of the same gradient-boosted tree buys almost nothing, because its errors correlate at 0.9 with the other fourteen. Adding one model with a genuinely different inductive bias — a linear model, a different feature set, a different horizon — lowers ρ, and lowering ρ is worth more than any number of near-duplicates.`};
  return o; });
}});

add({id:"tn-labelhorizon", dom:"Machine learning", vol:"06.2", name:"Set the label horizon", t:[150,180,210], gen(R){
  return mk(R, R=>{
  const T=pick(R,[500,1500,4000]);
  const noiseFloor=pick(R,[3,6,12,22]);   /* horizons below this are dominated by microstructure noise */
  /* Signal-to-noise per label rises with the horizon and saturates, because at
     very short horizons the move is mostly bid-ask bounce. Statistical power is
     that ratio times the root of the EFFECTIVE sample, which falls as 1/h. */
  const snrAt=h=>h/(h+noiseFloor);
  const util=h=>snrAt(h)*Math.sqrt(T/h)*0.6;
  const o={
    stem:`You are choosing the horizon over which to form labels on <b>${T.toLocaleString("en-US")}</b> observations. Below roughly <b>${noiseFloor} periods</b> the move is mostly bid-ask bounce, so signal-to-noise per label <em>rises</em> with the horizon. But adjacent labels then overlap more, so the <b>effective</b> sample shrinks as T/h.`,
    goal:`Choose the <b>label horizon</b> that maximises statistical power. This is the trade-off nobody makes deliberately.`,
    hint:"Signal per label grows with the horizon; effective sample size falls as 1/horizon. Power depends on both.",
    controls:[{n:"h", label:"Label horizon", lo:1, hi:60, step:1, init:5, unit:" periods", dec:0,
      note:"Periods ahead over which the label is formed."}],
    readout(v){
      const nEff=T/v.h;
      return [
        {l:"signal-to-noise", v:f2(snrAt(v.h),3), tone:snrAt(v.h)<0.4?"warn":"pos"},
        {l:"effective sample", v:f2(nEff,0), tone:nEff<80?"neg":nEff<250?"warn":"pos"},
        {l:"t-stat inflation if ignored", v:f2(Math.sqrt(v.h),1)+"×", tone:v.h>9?"neg":""},
        {l:"power score", v:f2(util(v.h),3)}];
    },
    chart(v){
      const vals=[]; for(let h=1;h<=60;h++) vals.push(util(h));
      return {vals, caption:`Statistical power against label horizon. Your setting: <b>${v.h} periods</b>.`};
    },
    score(v){ return util(v.h); },
    pass:0.88,
    sol:`Power is signal-to-noise times the root of the effective sample. The two pull opposite ways and cross at about <b>${noiseFloor} periods</b> — the point where the microstructure noise has been outgrown but the sample has not yet been spent.`,
    close:`The horizon is usually chosen from the strategy's intended holding period, which is reasonable, and it has a statistical consequence nobody costs: <b>every t-statistic you compute is inflated by √h</b> unless you weight by uniqueness.<br>
At a twenty-period label that is a factor of 4.5, which turns an unremarkable t of 0.9 into an apparently decisive 4. That single correction explains a large share of financial machine-learning results that fail to replicate — and it costs nothing to apply.`};
  return o; });
}});

/* ================= CRYPTO AND RATES ================= */

add({id:"tn-perpsize", dom:"Crypto", vol:"14.5", name:"Size a levered perpetual", t:[155,185,215], gen(R){
  return mk(R, R=>{
  const vol=pick(R,[50,80,130]);
  const mm=pick(R,[0.5,1,2.5]);
  const days=pick(R,[7,30,90]);
  const sigT=vol/100*Math.sqrt(days/365);
  /* total funding collected over the whole holding period at 1x, as a fraction */
  const fundTotal=rf(R,0.006,0.05,4);
  const funding=fundTotal/days*100;       /* % per day, for display */
  const bufferAt=lev=>Math.max(0.0005, 1/lev - mm/100);
  const pLiqAt=lev=>Math.min(0.98, 2*(1-Phi(bufferAt(lev)/sigT)));
  /* liquidation forfeits the margin and the position, so it costs about 1 */
  const util=lev=>fundTotal*lev - pLiqAt(lev);
  const o={
    stem:`A perpetual on a <b>${vol}%</b>-volatility asset, held for <b>${days}</b> days, with maintenance margin of <b>${f2(mm,1)}%</b>. Funding pays you <b>${f2(funding,2)}%</b> a day on the notional.<br>Over ${days} days the asset typically moves <b>${pc(sigT*100,0)}</b>.`,
    goal:`Choose the <b>leverage</b> that maximises expected return net of the probability of being liquidated. Liquidation does not merely cost the drawdown — it ends the position at the worst price.`,
    hint:"Your buffer to liquidation is 1/leverage − maintenance. Compare it against how far the asset moves over the holding period.",
    controls:[{n:"lev", label:"Leverage", lo:1, hi:25, step:0.5, init:3, unit:"×", dec:1,
      note:"Notional over margin posted."}],
    readout(v){
      const buffer=bufferAt(v.lev), pLiq=pLiqAt(v.lev);
      return [
        {l:"buffer to liquidation", v:pc(buffer*100,1), tone:buffer<sigT?"neg":""},
        {l:"P(liquidated)", v:pc(pLiq*100,0), tone:pLiq>0.3?"neg":pLiq>0.1?"warn":"pos"},
        {l:"funding earned", v:pc(fundTotal*v.lev*100,2), tone:"pos"},
        {l:"net objective", v:f2(util(v.lev),3), tone:util(v.lev)>0?"pos":"neg"}];
    },
    chart(v){
      const vals=[]; for(let x=1;x<=25.001;x+=0.5) vals.push(util(x));
      return {vals, base:0, caption:`Expected return net of liquidation risk. Your setting: <b>${f2(v.lev,1)}×</b>.`};
    },
    score(v){ return util(v.lev); },
    pass:0.9,
    sol:`The buffer goes as <b>1/leverage − maintenance</b>, and the asset moves <b>${pc(sigT*100,0)}</b> over this horizon. Where those cross is where the position becomes a coin flip.`,
    close:`The number that should govern this decision is the one on the left of the readout: <b>how far the asset moves over your holding period, against how far it can move before you are liquidated</b>. At ${vol}% volatility over ${days} days that is ${pc(sigT*100,0)}, and any leverage whose buffer is smaller than that is a position you will probably not still hold at the end.<br>
Note what the low maintenance margins on crypto venues actually buy you. A ${f2(mm,1)}% requirement permits enormous leverage, and permission is not advice — the buffer still shrinks as 1/leverage, so 20× leaves about ${pc((1/20-mm/100)*100,1)} of room in an asset that routinely moves several times that in a day.`};
  return o; });
}});

add({id:"tn-durgap", dom:"Rates", vol:"13A.7", name:"Set the duration of a bond book", t:[150,180,210], gen(R){
  return mk(R, R=>{
  const slope=rf(R,0.03,0.28,3);          /* % of yield per year of duration */
  const yvol=rf(R,0.5,1.6,2);             /* annual yield volatility, % */
  const tol=pick(R,[4,8,15]);             /* tolerable annual return volatility, % */
  const ret=d=>slope*d;
  const risk=d=>d*yvol;
  const util=d=>ret(d) - Math.max(0,(risk(d)-tol))*0.9 - 0.02*d*d*0.02;
  const o={
    stem:`The curve pays about <b>${f2(slope,3)}%</b> of extra yield per year of duration. Yields move with a volatility of <b>${f2(yvol,2)}%</b> a year, so each year of duration adds <b>${f2(yvol,2)}%</b> of return volatility. You can tolerate about <b>${tol}%</b>.`,
    goal:`Choose the <b>portfolio duration</b> that maximises expected return within your risk tolerance.`,
    hint:"Return rises linearly with duration. So does volatility, and past your tolerance the penalty bites hard.",
    controls:[{n:"d", label:"Portfolio duration", lo:0.5, hi:25, step:0.5, init:5, unit:" years", dec:1,
      note:"Modified duration of the book."}],
    readout(v){
      return [
        {l:"expected extra yield", v:pc(ret(v.d),2), tone:"pos"},
        {l:"return volatility", v:pc(risk(v.d),1), tone:risk(v.d)>tol?"neg":risk(v.d)>tol*0.85?"warn":"pos"},
        {l:"your tolerance", v:pc(tol,0)},
        {l:"objective", v:f2(util(v.d),3)}];
    },
    chart(v){
      const vals=[]; for(let x=0.5;x<=25.001;x+=0.5) vals.push(util(x));
      return {vals, base:0, caption:`Objective against duration. Your setting: <b>${f2(v.d,1)} years</b>.`};
    },
    score(v){ return util(v.d); },
    pass:0.9,
    sol:`Duration buys yield linearly and buys volatility linearly, so the constraint is what binds: <b>${tol}% ÷ ${f2(yvol,2)}% = ${f2(tol/yvol,1)} years</b>.`,
    close:`Unlike most of these surfaces, this one has no interior optimum from the return side — duration pays linearly, so more is always better until the constraint binds. That makes it a clean illustration of a general point: <b>when the objective is linear, the whole decision is the constraint</b>, and the work is in stating the constraint honestly.<br>
Convexity is the term this simple model omits, and it works in your favour: a long bond gains more when yields fall than it loses when they rise. That is why the true optimum sits slightly beyond where the linear arithmetic puts it, and why it is worth paying for on a book that will hold through large moves.`};
  return o; });
}});

