/* ============================================================
   OPTION BALANCING — part 1 of 3.

   An audit of the judgement bank found that the correct option was
   the longest in 93% of items, against a 25% chance baseline. That
   is a surface tell: a learner could score well by always choosing
   the longest option, and would be learning the tell rather than
   the material — while the accuracy statistics quietly measured
   nothing.

   The cause was structural. Correct options carried their own
   justification ("— at 18% participation you are a large part of
   the VWAP") while distractors were bare claims. The fix is to put
   every option on the same footing: each states a position, none
   argues for itself, and the reasoning lives in the solution,
   where it was already written.

   These modules rewrite `opts` in place, keyed by the first 55
   characters of the question. Questions, correct answers and
   solutions are untouched.
   ============================================================ */
